cular, it remains an open problem whether a factorial upper bound obtained for the three subclasses chordal bipartite graphs in the next three theorems can be strengthened to an implicit representation.\n\nTheorem 19\n\nThe class of X-free chordal bipartite graphs is factorial.\n\nProof\n\nLet G be a connected X-free chordal bipartite graph, let v be a vertex of G, and let 𝑉𝑖 be the set of vertices of G at distance i from v. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThese axioms mirror the behavior of knot diagrams under *Reidemeister moves*, the local transformations that describe when two diagrams represent the same knot. Each knot can be represented by a two-dimensional diagram with crossing information preserved. Reidemeister moves describe how we can deform such diagrams without changing the knot itself. (https://conferinte.stiu.md/sites/default/files/evenimente/Abstracts_ConfQRS_2025.pdf)\nciteturn5search12 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 1.3 years ago; The fundamental quandle is a *complete invariant*: two knots have isomorphic fundamental quandles if and only if they are equivalent (see [1]). ... In the following theorem, we characterize the finite quandles that admit only trivial colorings for every knot: ... To prove the equivalence of (1) and (2), we showed that for every finite connected quandle \\(Q\\) of size greater than 1, there exists a knot \\(K\\) such that \\(\\mathrm{Col}_Q(K) > |Q|\\).\nIn the following theorem, we characterize the finite quandles that admit only trivial colorings for every knot:\n\n**Theorem 1.** *Let \\(Q\\) be a finite quandle. The following are equivalent:*\n\n1. *For all knots \\(K\\), we have \\(\\mathrm{Col}_Q(K)=|Q|\\),*\n2. *\\(Q\\) is reductive,*\n3. *\\(\\mathrm{Col}_Q(K)\\) is a Vassiliev invariant.*\n\nBefore explaining the proof, we define *reductive* quandles. A quandle \\(Q\\) is called reductive if every connected subquandle of \\(Q\\) has size 1. A quandle is *connected* if the group of inner automorphisms, generated by left translations, acts transitively on \\(Q\\). Fundamental quandles are connected, and this property is preserved under quandle homomorphisms [1].\n\nTo prove the equivalence of (1) and (2), we showed that for every finite connected quandle \\(Q\\) of size greater than 1, there exists a knot \\(K\\) such that \\(\\mathrm{Col}_Q(K) > |Q|\\). This construction adapts ideas from the group-theoretic setting discussed in [2]. We then applied the characterization of reductive quandles from [3], which completes the equivalence (1) \\(\\Leftrightarrow\\) (2).\n\nFor the implication (2) \\(\\Leftrightarrow\\) (3), we followed the argument in [4]. A key lemma states that if a Vassiliev invariant is bounded in terms of the braid index, then it must be constant on all knots. Since \\(\\mathrm{Col}_Q(K)\\) is constant if and only if all colorings are trivial, we conclude that \\(\\mathrm{Col}_Q(K)\\) is a Vassiliev invariant if and only if \\(Q\\) is reductive.\n\nThe results presented in this abstract are part of author’s bachelor’s thesis [5], where the full proofs, additional examples, and broader context of the problem are provided. A preprint is currently being prepared.\n\n8--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFinite phylogenetic complexity and combinatorics of tables (https://msp.org/ant/2017/11-1/ant-v11-n1-p06-p.pdf)\nciteturn5search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.6 years ago; Conjecture 4.3 [Michałek 2013, Conjecture 9.3]. φ(G, n + 1) = max(2, φ(G, n)). ... The phylogenetic\ncomplexity of G = Z2 × Z2 is 4.\nfunction φ(G, · ) is eventually constant. The ensuing result would be a desired\nstrengthening of ours.\nConjecture 4.3 [Michałek 2013, Conjecture 9.3]. φ(G, n + 1) = max(2, φ(G, n)).\nWe are grateful to Seth Sullivant for noticing that this is equivalent to φ(G, · )\nbeing constant, apart from the case when G = Z2 and n = 3, when the associated\nvariety is the whole proje